steps.text:
Yes — that’s a bug/limitation in Tailwind v0.1.3’s `@apply` handling.

`border-dotted` is deliberately emitted twice in the utility stylesheet:

```css
.border-dotted { border-width: 0; } /* reset */
.border { border: 1px solid …; }    /* width utility */
.border-dotted { border-style: dotted; }
```

So this works in markup when combined with `border`:

```html
<div class="border border-dotted"></div>
```

The stylesheet order restores the width before applying the dotted style.

But `@apply border-dotted` copies both matching `.border-dotted` rules into your selector, including `border-width: 0`, and it does not also bring in `.border`. Result:

```css
.your-class {
  border-width: 0;
  border-style: dotted;
}
```

Use:

```css
.your-class {
  @apply border border-dotted;
}
```

(The older syntax may also accept `@apply .border .border-dotted;`.)

So your diagnosis of `borderStylesReset` is correct: it was designed for composed utility classes in the generated stylesheet, but it breaks the expectation that `@apply border-dotted` is independently usable.
